---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'How to Outfit a Construction Crew: A Practical Plan',
 description:
 'A practical planning guide for construction crew apparel. Review sizing, employer-approved safety gear, decoration, timing, and ordering workflows.',
 category: 'Crew Outfitting',
 readTime: '9 min read',
 date: 'Mar 1, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-03-01',
 image: '/images/og-default.jpg',
 slug: 'how-to-outfit-a-construction-crew-vegas',
}

const faqs = [
 { q: 'What should you decide first when outfitting a construction crew?', a: 'Start with the employer\'s hazard assessment and current site requirements. The safety lead selects any required protective garments and performance classes before decoration.' },
 { q: 'What apparel categories might a construction crew use?', a: 'A role-based program may include field wear, employer-approved hi-vis garments, office apparel, outerwear, and headwear. Roles, shifts, laundry, and weather set the quantities.' },
 { q: 'Should you bulk order or evaluate a company store for a construction crew?', a: 'Compare crew size, turnover, approvals, and delivery needs. Bulk ordering suits one-time outfitting; a company store suits ongoing hiring.' },
 { q: 'What artwork files help with decorated crew apparel?', a: 'A vector file such as .ai, .eps, or .svg is often helpful, plus a single-color and simplified small-format version. Your quote lists the artwork and file requirements.' },
 { q: 'How can you reduce rush-order risk on crew apparel?', a: 'Forecast hiring and seasonal needs early, submit approved artwork before the order window, and ask for current product availability and partner capacity. Do not rely on unconfirmed inventory or timing.' },
 { q: 'When should a contractor consider a company store?', a: 'Consider a store when recurring orders, approvals, and size collection create measurable admin work. Your written setup plan covers user access, allowances, reporting, payment, and delivery.' },
]

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
 {
 '@type': 'Article',
 headline: post.title,
 description: post.description,
 image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`,
 datePublished: post.isoDate,
 dateModified: '2026-08-21',
 author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/about/' },
 publisher: {
 '@type': 'Organization',
 name: 'Bighorn Threads',
 logo: {
 '@type': 'ImageObject',
 url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png',
 },
 },
 mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 },
 {
 '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
 itemListElement: [
 { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
 { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/blog/' },
 {
 '@type': 'ListItem',
 position: 3,
 name: post.title,
 item: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 },
 ],
 },
 {
 '@type': 'FAQPage',
 mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
 '@type': 'Question',
 name: f.q,
 acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
 })),
 },
 {
 '@type': 'WebPage',
 url: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['h1','.speakable-answer'] },
 },
 ],
}
const howToSchema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@type': 'HowTo',
 name: 'How to Outfit a Construction Crew in Las Vegas',
 description: 'A practical six-step planning guide for Las Vegas construction and trades crew apparel.',
 step: [
 { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Start with your jobsite requirements', text: 'Obtain the employer\'s hazard assessment and current site requirements. The safety lead selects any protective garment and performance class.' },
 { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Choose your apparel categories', text: 'Build a category list: daily field wear (moisture-wicking hi-vis tees), safety layer (vests), office and sales (embroidered polos), outerwear (softshells and jackets), and headwear (branded hats).' },
 { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Decide between bulk ordering and a company store', text: 'Compare recurring order volume, approvals, size collection, payment, reporting, production, fulfillment, and delivery needs before selecting a workflow.' },
 { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Get your artwork ready', text: 'Prepare a vector file (.ai, .eps, .svg), a single-color version for hi-vis tees, and a simplified small-format version for hats and left-chest embroidery.' },
 { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Plan timing and turnaround', text: 'Share the requested products, sizes, approved artwork, quantity, and deadline early. Get stock and capacity confirmed in writing.' },
 { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Scope a company store if appropriate', text: 'Document required user access, HR integration, ordering, payment, reporting, fulfillment, and delivery capabilities and confirm them in writing before setup.' },
 ],
}
---

<BaseLayout
 title={post.title}
 description={post.description}
 canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`}
 ogImage={post.image}
 type="article"
 publishedDate={post.isoDate}
>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(howToSchema)} />

 <!-- Hero -->
 <article class="bg-concrete">
 <header class="border-b border-hairline bg-paper">

 <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-20 pb-12 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24 lg:px-8">
 <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-ash" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
 <a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Home</a>
 <span class="px-2">/</span>
 <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Blog</a>
 </nav>

 <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">
 {post.category}
 </span>
 <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">
 {post.title}
 </h1>
 <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">
 Sizing chaos. Rush orders. That back closet full of XL shirts nobody will ever wear. Here's how to outfit a crew the right way.
 </p>

 <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash">
 <span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span>
 <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
 <span>{post.readTime}</span>
 <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
 <time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time>
 </div>
 </div>
 </header>

 <!-- Featured image -->
 <!-- Body -->
 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
 <!-- Table of contents (sticky on desktop) -->
 <aside class="mb-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6 lg:sticky lg:top-24">
 <h2 class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">In this article</h2>
 <ol class="space-y-2 font-mono text-sm text-charcoal">
 <li><a href="#jobsite-needs" class="hover:text-gold-700">1. Start with your jobsite needs</a></li>
 <li><a href="#apparel-categories" class="hover:text-gold-700">2. Choose your apparel categories</a></li>
 <li><a href="#bulk-vs-store" class="hover:text-gold-700">3. Bulk order vs company store</a></li>
 <li><a href="#artwork" class="hover:text-gold-700">4. Get your artwork right</a></li>
 <li><a href="#timing" class="hover:text-gold-700">5. Timing & turnaround</a></li>
 <li><a href="#vegas-example" class="hover:text-gold-700">6. A company store in practice</a></li>
 </ol>
 </aside>

 <p class="speakable-answer mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Outfitting a crew means sizes, roles, garments, artwork, and a date that won’t move. Most of the trouble comes from those decisions living in different places.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 This guide walks through the six decisions that shape a crew apparel program.
 </p>

 <h2 id="jobsite-needs" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">1. Start With Your Jobsite Needs</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Before you pick protective apparel, obtain the employer's hazard assessment and current site specification. The safety lead selects the required product and rating.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">OSHA and hi-vis class requirements</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 The employer selects high-visibility apparel from the hazard assessment, traffic-control plan, contracts, and current site rules. Do not select Class 2 or Class 3 from speed, shift, or job title alone.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Vegas weather reality</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Las Vegas heat makes fabric weight, fit, and layering important. Compare employer-approved garments by season and task. Clothing choices do not replace the employer's heat safety program.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border-l-4 border-gold-500 bg-gold-500/5 p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Key takeaway</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Build the approved apparel standard before the first order. Recheck current product labels, availability, and site requirements before each reorder.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="apparel-categories" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">2. Choose Your Apparel Categories</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A crew program may use several categories. Choose only what fits the roles, job requirements, budget, and approved product specification:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Daily field wear.</strong> Choose quantities from shifts, laundry, weather, onboarding, and replacement needs.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Safety layer.</strong> Use only the employer-approved performance class and exact garment.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Office & sales.</strong> Embroidered polos and button-downs for the people walking into client offices. Different garment, different decoration method, same brand identity.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Outerwear.</strong> Compare softshells, insulated jackets, and hoodies by role, weather, care, and approved use.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Headwear.</strong> Confirm style, fit, jobsite restrictions, and decoration before ordering.</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Our <a href="/services/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">services page</a> covers each category if you need a starting point.
 </p>

 <h2 id="bulk-vs-store" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">3. Bulk Order vs Company Store</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Compare bulk ordering with a company-store workflow using recurring volume, turnover, size collection, approvals, storage, payment, and delivery needs.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Larger or high-turnover crews may evaluate a <a href="/company-stores/contractors-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">company store</a>. See <a href="/blog/company-store-vs-bulk-ordering/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">Bulk Ordering vs Company Store</a> for planning questions.
 </p>


 <h2 id="artwork" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">4. Get Your Artwork Right</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 The garment and method set the artwork needs. These files can help:
 </p>

 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Vector file</strong> (.ai, .eps, or .svg) when requested by the production partner.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Single-color version</strong>: for hi-vis tees, where full color can look muddy.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Small-format version</strong>: simplified for hats and left-chest embroidery where detail gets lost.</li>
 </ol>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Then compare permitted decoration methods by garment, artwork, quantity, finish, care, and budget. Read our <a href="/screen-printing-vs-embroidery-construction-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">screen printing vs embroidery guide</a>.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Pro tip</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Send us your logo before you need to order. Bighorn handles embroidery digitizing and screen print preparation. Review the current setup and file terms in the written quote.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="timing" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">5. Timing & Turnaround</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Artwork approval, stock, and quantity drive the timing. A quoted rush option may be available. Reduce deadline risk with these steps:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Onboarding bundles.</strong> Set a starter-kit quantity from hiring forecasts, approved products, storage, and current inventory.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Seasonal planning.</strong> Forecast summer and winter needs early, then confirm current supplier availability.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Reorder triggers.</strong> Track recurring needs internally. Automated triggers require written store-capability confirmation.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="vegas-example" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">6. How a Company Store Plays Out in Practice</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Picture a contractor running several projects at once. Sizes live in a foreman’s notebook, each job buys from a different vendor, and the old logo keeps turning up. Write down how that works today before you compare it to a store.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A company store may help organize approved catalogs and employee sizes. Ask about access tiers, allowances, onboarding emails, and HR integration, and get what your store will do in writing before setup.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Judge a store by how much admin it actually removes. Get inventory, budgets, and reports in the written setup plan before you count on them.
 </p>

 <!-- Mid-article CTA -->
 <div class="my-12 border border-hairline bg-gold-500/10 p-8 text-center">
 <h3 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Need help outfitting your crew?</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-charcoal">
 Bighorn runs the account from Las Vegas and gets the approved gear made. Tell us the crew size and requirements to request a plan.
 </p>
